export default [
academic('01','The value of negative results',`A scientific experiment does not become informative only when it confirms a prediction. Suppose researchers test whether a coating makes a material more durable. If coated and uncoated samples perform similarly, the result can limit claims about the coating. It does not necessarily prove that the coating could never work: the test may cover only one temperature or one kind of wear.\nNegative results are difficult to interpret when the procedure itself is uncertain. An insensitive measuring instrument might fail to detect a small improvement. Researchers therefore need to report how samples were selected, what was measured, and how precise the measurements were. These details help others distinguish evidence of little effect from a study unable to identify an effect.\nPublication practices can also shape knowledge. If journals publish mainly positive findings, a reader may see an apparently consistent pattern while missing unsuccessful tests. Recording well-designed negative studies gives later researchers a more complete basis for deciding which explanations deserve further attention.`,[
q('Main Idea','What is the passage mainly arguing?',["Well-documented negative results can contribute to scientific understanding", "Careful instrument design is the main way to reduce the publication of unsuccessful experiments", "Negative findings are chiefly useful for deciding which coating should be marketed", "A failed coating test establishes the general durability of the uncoated material"],'Recording well-designed negative studies','The value depends on design and reporting, rather than on whether a prediction is confirmed.'),
q('Factual','Why might a study miss a small improvement?',['Its measuring instrument may be insufficiently sensitive','Its samples were coated','Its findings were published','Its researchers described the temperature'],'An insensitive measuring instrument might fail to detect a small improvement','Measurement sensitivity is the stated limitation.'),
q('Inference','What can an unsuccessful test at one temperature establish?',['A limit on claims under the tested conditions','That the coating fails under every temperature','That uncoated materials cannot be durable','That publication practices have no influence'],'the test may cover only one temperature','The evidence applies to a limited setting and cannot support universal failure.'),
q('Reference','What does “These details” refer to?',['Information about sampling, measurement, and precision','The coating’s advertising claims','Only the number of journal readers','The positive findings of other researchers'],'how samples were selected, what was measured, and how precise the measurements were','The phrase points to the reporting information in the preceding sentence.'),
q('Purpose','Why discuss journals’ preference for positive findings?',['To explain how the available record can become incomplete','To argue that all positive results are fabricated','To recommend abandoning material tests','To show that temperature controls publication'],'missing unsuccessful tests','Selective publication can make an apparent pattern stronger than the full evidence warrants.')]),
academic('02','How a shoreline records change',`A shoreline drawn on a map may appear to be a fixed boundary, but the water's edge moves with tides, weather, and sediment transport. Comparing two maps can therefore suggest a change that partly reflects different conditions at the time of observation. A careful comparison uses a consistent definition of the shoreline and records when each measurement was made.\nSediment does not simply move from land toward the sea. Waves approaching at an angle can carry sand along a coast, redistributing it between neighbouring beaches. A structure that traps sand in one place may leave less available farther along the shore. The benefit at a protected beach must therefore be considered alongside changes elsewhere.\nLong observation periods are especially useful because a storm may remove sand that later returns during calmer conditions. A measurement made immediately after the storm could exaggerate a lasting loss. Researchers combine repeated surveys with information about waves and tides to distinguish short-term variation from a persistent trend.`,[
q('Main Idea','Which statement best captures the passage?',["Shoreline change must be interpreted using consistent and repeated observations", "Storm erosion is the principal cause of lasting shoreline retreat", "Coastal protection is most effective when sand is trapped near a single beach", "Using the newest map removes the need to compare observation conditions"],'distinguish short-term variation from a persistent trend','The passage explains why definitions, conditions, and time span matter.'),
q('Factual','How can waves redistribute sand between beaches?',['By carrying it along the coast','By removing only the largest grains','By fixing the water’s edge permanently','By reducing all tidal movement'],'carry sand along a coast','Angled waves support movement alongshore, not only offshore.'),
q('Inference','What might happen beyond a structure that traps sand?',['Another beach may receive less sediment','Every beach will become wider','Tides will cease to affect mapping','The structure will eliminate future storms'],'leave less available farther along the shore','Local sediment capture can affect supply to neighbouring areas.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “persistent” mean in the final sentence?',['Continuing over time','Immediately visible','Measured without tides','Occurring only once'],'a persistent trend','It contrasts with short-term variation rather than with visible change.'),
q('Purpose','Why mention sand returning after a storm?',['To illustrate how a temporary loss can be mistaken for a lasting one','To prove that storms never change coasts','To recommend surveying only in calm weather','To explain why maps use different colours'],'sand that later returns during calmer conditions','A single post-storm measurement can misrepresent longer-term change.')]),
academic('03','The work of a dictionary',`Dictionaries are sometimes treated as authorities that decide which words people may use. In practice, many dictionaries describe usage by examining evidence from speech and writing. Editors gather examples, identify recurring meanings, and decide whether a new use is sufficiently established to include. Entry does not imply that every speaker approves of the word.\nThe evidence available to editors is uneven. Published texts are easier to collect than informal conversations, and some communities produce more searchable writing than others. A word may therefore be common in daily speech before it appears frequently in a written collection. Editors can address this imbalance by drawing on different kinds of material, although no collection represents every speaker equally.\nDefinitions also require judgment. A familiar word can develop a new meaning gradually, making it difficult to decide where one sense ends and another begins. Examples help readers see how a word operates in context. The dictionary is thus a carefully organized account of language evidence, rather than a complete inventory of all possible expression.`,[
q('Main Idea','How does the author characterize dictionaries?',["As organized interpretations of evidence about language use", "Dictionary inclusion gives a word an approved meaning that speakers should follow", "Published language is more representative than informal speech because it is easier to collect", "Changes in word meaning are resolved by choosing the most familiar definition"],'a carefully organized account of language evidence','Editors use evidence and judgment; neither arbitrary rules nor complete coverage describes the process.'),
q('Factual','Why may spoken words be underrepresented?',['Informal conversation is harder to collect than published text','Speech contains fewer meanings than writing','Editors refuse to examine spoken material','Every community writes equally often'],'Published texts are easier to collect than informal conversations','The difference concerns evidence availability.'),
q('Inference','What does a word’s inclusion not necessarily show?',['Universal approval among speakers','Evidence of an established use','A decision by editors to describe it','Examples of recurring meanings'],'does not imply that every speaker approves','Inclusion records usage rather than unanimous endorsement.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “sense” mean in the last paragraph?',['A distinct meaning of a word','A speaker’s physical perception','A dictionary’s publication date','The sound of a conversation'],'where one sense ends and another begins','The discussion concerns boundaries between related meanings.'),
q('Purpose','Why are examples useful in an entry?',['They show how a meaning functions in context','They eliminate all editorial judgment','They prove that every speaker uses a word','They replace the need to collect evidence'],'Examples help readers see how a word operates in context','Context illustrates a sense without claiming complete representativeness.')]),
academic('04','Paying attention to a soundscape',`An environment contains more than isolated sounds. The combination of voices, machines, animals, and moving air creates what researchers call a soundscape. People may evaluate the same sound differently depending on what they are doing. Conversation can make a public square feel lively but distract someone trying to study beside it.\nMeasuring sound intensity is useful, yet it does not capture every aspect of an experience. A steady ventilation hum and an irregular series of sharp noises can have similar average intensity while attracting different amounts of attention. Timing, frequency, and the meaning assigned to a sound all influence its effect. A familiar bell may convey helpful information even when it is louder than background noise.\nDesigning a comfortable environment therefore involves more than reducing a single number. Planners can consider where activities occur, when noises arise, and whether users can choose quieter spaces. Asking users about their experience complements physical measurements. Together, these approaches reveal why an intervention that improves one location might be less helpful in another.`,[
q('Main Idea','What does the passage emphasize about managing sound?',["Context and user experience matter alongside intensity measurements", "Reducing average sound intensity is the most reliable way to improve each location", "Irregular noises should be replaced with steady sounds regardless of users’ activities", "Public sound design should chiefly increase the availability of informative bells"],'involves more than reducing a single number','The author combines physical measures with timing, meaning, and activity.'),
q('Factual','Which feature may differ between sounds with similar average intensity?',['How much attention they attract','Whether they can be measured at all','Their guaranteed effect on all listeners','Whether they form part of an environment'],'attracting different amounts of attention','Steady and irregular sounds can have similar averages but different attentional effects.'),
q('Inference','Why might conversation be welcome in one setting and disruptive in another?',['The activities and expectations of listeners differ','Its physical intensity cannot change','Only squares contain voices','Study spaces do not have soundscapes'],'depending on what they are doing','The same sound is evaluated through the listener’s activity.'),
q('Reference','What does “these approaches” in the final paragraph include?',['User reports and physical sound measurements','Only the use of quieter ventilation','The removal of bells and voices','A comparison of two average intensity values'],'Asking users about their experience complements physical measurements','The two complementary evidence sources are named immediately before the phrase.'),
q('Purpose','Why does the author use the example of a familiar bell?',['To show that a louder sound can serve a useful purpose','To prove bells are quieter than background noise','To argue that intensity is impossible to measure','To recommend bells in every study room'],'may convey helpful information','Meaning can make a sound useful despite its volume.')]),
academic('05','When a model is useful',`A model represents selected features of a system rather than reproducing every detail. A simple model of traffic might describe how many vehicles enter a junction each minute while ignoring differences among drivers. Whether this simplification is acceptable depends on the question being asked. Estimating average congestion may require less detail than investigating how a particular collision occurred.\nAdding complexity does not automatically improve a model. More variables require additional measurements, and uncertain values can introduce uncertain predictions. A complicated model can also be difficult to inspect, making errors harder to detect. Researchers often begin with a simpler version and add detail when comparisons with observations reveal a specific need.\nA useful model should therefore be evaluated against its purpose and evidence. Agreement with one set of observations is encouraging, but testing it on other conditions offers a stronger check. When a prediction fails, the mismatch may reveal an omitted process. Models help organize reasoning precisely because their assumptions and limitations can be examined rather than hidden.`,[
q('Main Idea','What is the author’s main claim?',["A model’s value depends on purpose, evidence, and inspectable assumptions", "Detailed models provide stronger evidence because they include more variables", "A model is useful mainly when its original observations closely match its assumptions", "Failed predictions show that a model needs more measurements rather than a revised process"],'evaluated against its purpose and evidence','The passage presents selective representation as useful when its limits are assessed.'),
q('Factual','What disadvantage can extra variables introduce?',['Greater uncertainty from additional measurements','A guarantee of simpler inspection','A complete removal of assumptions','Less need for observations'],'uncertain values can introduce uncertain predictions','Additional inputs can carry uncertainty rather than automatically improving accuracy.'),
q('Inference','Which traffic question may demand more detail?',['How a specific collision happened','The average number of vehicles waiting','The average flow through a junction','Whether congestion is broadly increasing'],'investigating how a particular collision occurred','Explaining an individual event may require features that an aggregate model omits.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “omitted” mean here?',['Left out','Directly measured','Made more accurate','Repeated unnecessarily'],'an omitted process','A failed prediction may indicate something missing from the representation.'),
q('Purpose','Why test a model under other conditions?',['To check whether its success extends beyond one observation set','To ensure it matches only its original data','To eliminate the need to state assumptions','To prove no simpler version could work'],'testing it on other conditions offers a stronger check','Broader testing probes usefulness beyond the initial agreement.')]),
academic('06','Preserving a performance',`Preserving a musical performance differs from preserving a painting. A painting remains as a physical object, whereas a performance unfolds through actions in time. A recording captures sound, but it may not show gestures, interaction, or the audience's response. Written notation preserves another aspect, yet it cannot specify every choice made by a performer.\nArchivists therefore combine materials rather than assume that one record is sufficient. Programmes identify participants and context, video can document movement, and interviews can explain decisions that are not obvious from a recording. Such accounts must still be interpreted carefully: memories change, and performers may describe intentions differently after an event.\nPreservation can also include teaching the practice to new participants. This sustains skills that a recording alone cannot transmit fully, although each new performance will differ. The aim is not necessarily to freeze one version forever. It may be to keep enough evidence and knowledge for future people to understand how the performance was created and why it mattered.`,[
q('Main Idea','What challenge does the passage describe?',["No single record captures every aspect of a performance", "Combining recordings produces a permanent version that future performers should reproduce", "Interviews are the most reliable records because performers can explain their intentions", "Teaching is chiefly a way to preserve the audience response to an earlier event"],'combine materials rather than assume that one record is sufficient','Different sources preserve different features of a time-based event.'),
q('Factual','What can interviews contribute?',['Explanations of decisions not clear in a recording','A guarantee that memories have not changed','A complete replacement for video','Identical future performances'],'explain decisions that are not obvious from a recording','Interviews supply interpretation but require critical treatment.'),
q('Inference','Why might archivists teach a performance practice?',['Some practical skills are difficult to transmit through recordings alone','Teaching prevents any variation','Written notation includes every gesture','Future participants need no historical context'],'skills that a recording alone cannot transmit fully','Living instruction can preserve abilities beyond the recorded sound.'),
q('Reference','What does “Such accounts” refer to?',['The explanations given in interviews','Paintings kept in an archive','Every written programme','The audience’s recorded applause'],'interviews can explain decisions','The caution about memory applies to interview accounts.'),
q('Purpose','Why contrast a painting with a performance?',['To introduce the difference between an enduring object and an event','To rank visual art above music','To argue that paintings change more rapidly','To propose replacing concerts with exhibitions'],'a performance unfolds through actions in time','The contrast explains why preservation methods must differ.')]),
academic('07','Finding order in a collection',`A museum collection can be arranged in several ways. Objects might be grouped by the place they came from, the material used to make them, or the activity they supported. Each arrangement highlights some relationships while making others less visible. A room of metal objects, for example, allows comparison of techniques across regions but may separate tools from the communities that used them.\nDigital catalogues can give an object several descriptive tags, allowing users to explore more than one arrangement. This flexibility does not remove the need for judgment. A tag reflects a decision about what feature matters, and older descriptions may use categories that a community now contests. Revising the catalogue can require consultation as well as technical editing.\nVisitors also bring different questions. Someone studying manufacture may want material classifications, while someone tracing family history may look for geographic connections. Providing several routes into the collection can serve both. No display is neutral simply because it is orderly; organization is part of the interpretation presented to the public.`,[
q('Main Idea','What is the central point about organizing museum objects?',["Organization shapes interpretation and can support different questions", "Material classifications provide the most useful interpretation for museum visitors", "Digital tags resolve the limitations of older catalogues by increasing technical flexibility", "Geographic arrangements should replace other classifications to restore community context"],'organization is part of the interpretation','Classification emphasizes selected relationships rather than remaining neutral.'),
q('Factual','What can material grouping make easier?',['Comparing techniques across regions','Keeping all objects from one community together','Removing contested descriptions automatically','Tracing every visitor’s family history'],'comparison of techniques across regions','The metal-object example identifies a benefit alongside a cultural-context cost.'),
q('Inference','Why might revising a catalogue require consultation?',['People may disagree with inherited categories','Tags cannot be changed technically','Objects lose their material properties','Every visitor uses the same research question'],'categories that a community now contests','Classification involves social judgment as well as data entry.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “contests” mean in paragraph 2?',['Challenges','Organizes into a competition','Records carefully','Adopts without question'],'a community now contests','The community disputes the categories rather than participating in a contest.'),
q('Purpose','Why describe two visitors with different interests?',['To show why multiple ways of exploring are useful','To prove geography is more useful than manufacture','To require every visitor to trace family history','To reject digital catalogues'],'Providing several routes into the collection can serve both','Different interests justify more than one organizational path.')]),
academic('08','Reading a tree ring',`In many temperate trees, growth produces a visible ring each year. The width and composition of rings can contain information about conditions experienced by the tree. A narrow ring might reflect dry weather, but it could also result from competition, disease, or damage. Interpreting it requires more than matching width to a single cause.\nResearchers strengthen an interpretation by comparing several trees from the same area. If many show unusually limited growth at the same time, a shared environmental influence becomes more plausible. A pattern found in only one tree may have a more local explanation. Even a shared pattern must be checked against what is known about the species and the site.\nOld wooden objects can sometimes preserve long records, but their history introduces complications. Timber may have been stored or reused before construction. The date represented by its final ring therefore need not be the date of the building in which it was found. Ring evidence becomes more informative when combined with records of construction and careful examination of the wood.`,[
q('Main Idea','What does the passage emphasize?',["Tree-ring evidence is valuable but must be interpreted with context", "Regional ring patterns directly identify the weather responsible for growth changes", "The final growth ring offers a practical date for the construction of a wooden building", "Comparing tree species is chiefly useful for choosing timber suitable for reuse"],'Interpreting it requires more than matching width to a single cause','Multiple influences and timber histories limit simple readings.'),
q('Factual','Which condition besides dryness can narrow a ring?',['Competition','Later reuse of the timber','The date of a building record','The number of researchers'],'competition, disease, or damage','These are listed influences on tree growth.'),
q('Inference','Why compare trees from one area?',['A common pattern may indicate a shared environmental influence','Every tree must have the same ring width','It eliminates the need to consider species','It proves that the timber was never stored'],'a shared environmental influence becomes more plausible','Comparison separates possible shared effects from individual disturbances.'),
q('Sentence Simplification','Which statement best restates the point about the final ring and building date?',['The wood may predate the building because it was stored or reused','A building is always older than the wood used in it','The final ring dates the first time the building was repaired','Stored wood stops containing growth information'],'Timber may have been stored or reused before construction','The paraphrase preserves the reason why ring date and construction date may differ.'),
q('Purpose','Why mention disease and damage?',['To show that narrow rings have alternative explanations','To suggest that ring studies should use only damaged trees','To explain why all trees form rings','To date the construction of wooden objects'],'it could also result from competition, disease, or damage','The alternatives caution against attributing every narrow ring to weather.')]),
academic('09','The timing of a decision',`People deciding whether to replace a household appliance may compare purchase prices while overlooking later running costs. A cheaper machine can use more electricity, making it more expensive over its lifetime. However, future savings do not help a household that cannot afford the larger initial payment. A decision that appears inefficient from a long-term perspective may reflect an immediate financial constraint.\nInformation can change the comparison but does not remove every barrier. Labels that estimate annual energy use make costs easier to compare. Yet actual expenses depend on how often the appliance runs and on future energy prices. Estimates are useful guides rather than promises of an exact saving.\nPolicies designed to encourage efficient purchases can address both information and access. A payment arrangement may help with the initial cost, while clear labels support comparison. Evaluating such a policy requires asking who can use it, not simply whether the efficient model is available. Availability in a shop and practical affordability are different conditions.`,[
q('Main Idea','What is the passage’s main point?',["Long-term value must be considered alongside information and immediate affordability", "Clear energy labels are sufficient to make efficient appliances accessible to households", "Lifetime running costs should determine a purchase regardless of the initial payment", "Payment arrangements matter primarily because they improve the precision of energy estimates"],'Availability in a shop and practical affordability are different conditions','The passage distinguishes lifetime efficiency from practical access.'),
q('Factual','What affects actual running expenses?',['Usage frequency and future energy prices','Only the initial purchase price','The number of models on display','The size of a label'],'how often the appliance runs and on future energy prices','The label is an estimate because these factors can vary.'),
q('Inference','Why might someone choose a less efficient machine?',['The more efficient one requires an unaffordable initial payment','They have been guaranteed lower lifetime costs','Energy use is identical for every household','The shop has no information labels'],'cannot afford the larger initial payment','An immediate constraint can explain a choice despite future savings.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “constraint” mean here?',['A limiting condition','An exact prediction','A preferred design','A future refund'],'an immediate financial constraint','The phrase describes a limit on what the household can pay now.'),
q('Purpose','Why distinguish availability from affordability?',['To show that policy evaluation must consider whether people can use an option','To recommend removing efficient machines from shops','To show labels are unnecessary','To prove that all payment arrangements fail'],'asking who can use it','An option’s presence alone does not establish access.')]),
academic('10','A boundary between habitats',`The edge between a forest and an open field is not merely a line on a map. Light, wind, and temperature can differ near the edge from conditions deeper inside the forest. These changes affect which plants grow and which animals can find suitable shelter. Some species benefit from the mixture of habitats, while others depend on interior conditions.\nWhen a large forest is divided into smaller patches, its total area may decline only modestly while the proportion exposed to edges increases substantially. Measuring area alone can therefore miss an important ecological change. Two patches of equal area can also have different amounts of edge if one is compact and the other long and narrow.\nConservation planning consequently considers shape and surrounding land use as well as size. A buffer of vegetation may soften some changes at a boundary, but it will not reproduce every feature of an undisturbed interior. The usefulness of a measure depends on which species and processes it is meant to protect.`,[
q('Main Idea','What should forest conservation consider besides area?',["The amount and conditions of habitat edges", "Compact forest patches benefit conservation mainly by reducing the amount of land required", "Vegetated buffers can substitute for the conditions of a forest interior", "Forest area is a sufficient planning measure when neighbouring patches have equal size"],'shape and surrounding land use as well as size','Edges create conditions not captured by area alone.'),
q('Factual','Which patch can have more edge for the same area?',['A long narrow patch','A compact patch in every case','One measured on a newer map','One with no surrounding land use'],'one is compact and the other long and narrow','Shape changes perimeter relative to area.'),
q('Inference','Why may modest area loss still matter greatly?',['Fragmentation can expose more of the remaining forest to edge conditions','Every interior species prefers bright light','The remaining patches stop containing trees','Buffers remove all boundary effects'],'the proportion exposed to edges increases substantially','The ecological effect can exceed what total area loss suggests.'),
q('Reference','What does “These changes” in the first paragraph refer to?',['Differences in light, wind, and temperature','Only the loss of forest on a map','Animal movement between continents','The addition of conservation buffers'],'Light, wind, and temperature can differ near the edge','The phrase points to the environmental differences just listed.'),
q('Purpose','Why qualify the benefit of a vegetation buffer?',['To explain that it cannot fully recreate interior conditions','To show that buffers always worsen edge effects','To eliminate species-specific planning','To argue that forest shape is irrelevant'],'will not reproduce every feature of an undisturbed interior','The qualification prevents treating a buffer as a complete substitute.')]),
academic('11','How standards coordinate work',`A standard can allow products made by different organizations to work together. If a connector has a common size and arrangement, users need not obtain every component from the same supplier. This can support competition and simplify replacement. Agreement is valuable because the usefulness of one product may depend on compatibility with many others.\nEstablishing a standard also involves choices. A design suitable for existing equipment may not be ideal for future devices. Changing it can improve performance but impose costs on people who already own incompatible products. Keeping it indefinitely may protect those investments while limiting new possibilities.\nStandards bodies therefore need procedures for evaluating proposals and managing transitions. An announced change can include a period during which both versions are supported. Clear identification helps users distinguish them. The technical specification matters, but so does how the change is communicated and adopted. Coordination depends on participants knowing which rules apply and when, rather than simply on publishing a document.`,[
q('Main Idea','What is the passage mainly about?',["The benefits and transition challenges of shared standards", "Shared standards mainly benefit users by preventing changes to existing designs", "A published technical specification is sufficient to coordinate a transition", "Competition among suppliers depends chiefly on replacing common connectors with proprietary designs"],'evaluating proposals and managing transitions','Standards enable compatibility but changes require deliberate coordination.'),
q('Factual','How can a common connector benefit users?',['It permits components from different suppliers to work together','It prevents replacement of damaged parts','It requires purchase from one manufacturer','It eliminates all future design choices'],'users need not obtain every component from the same supplier','Compatibility reduces reliance on a single source.'),
q('Inference','Why might organizations support two versions temporarily?',['To ease the move from existing equipment to a new standard','To hide which rules apply','To prevent any performance improvement','To make both versions permanently indistinguishable'],'a period during which both versions are supported','A transition period reduces disruption while adoption occurs.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “impose” mean in the second paragraph?',['Create or require people to bear','Measure precisely','Pay on someone’s behalf','Avoid completely'],'impose costs on people','A change can place a financial burden on existing owners.'),
q('Purpose','Why mention communication alongside specification?',['Successful coordination requires users to understand adoption rules','The technical design is always unimportant','Participants cannot read documents','All changes must be delayed indefinitely'],'knowing which rules apply and when','Implementation depends on shared understanding as well as the written design.')]),
academic('12','Waiting and perceived service',`Waiting time has a measurable duration, but people do not judge service solely by counting minutes. Uncertainty can make a wait feel longer. A passenger who knows when the next bus is expected can plan differently from one who sees no information, even if both eventually wait the same amount of time.\nProgress information can help, provided it is credible. An estimate that changes without explanation may increase frustration rather than reduce it. Giving a precise time is not always better than giving a realistic range, especially when conditions are variable. Explaining a delay can also distinguish an unavoidable disruption from an impression that staff are ignoring users.\nThis does not mean information substitutes for improving service. A display cannot create a bus that has not arrived. Instead, operational improvements and communication address different aspects of the experience. Evaluating a change may therefore require both actual timing data and reports of how confidently users could make decisions during the wait.`,[
q('Main Idea','What is the author’s main point?',["Credible information affects waiting experience but does not replace better service", "Accurate displays improve service mainly by shortening the physical waiting period", "Providing a precise estimate is preferable to explaining the uncertainty of a delay", "Passenger reports should replace actual timing data when evaluating a service change"],'operational improvements and communication address different aspects','The author distinguishes physical service performance from perceived uncertainty.'),
q('Factual','What can increase frustration?',['An estimate that changes without explanation','A realistic range under variable conditions','An explanation of an unavoidable delay','Information that lets passengers plan'],'An estimate that changes without explanation','Credibility matters; information is not automatically beneficial.'),
q('Inference','When might a range be preferable to one precise time?',['When arrival conditions vary','When no passengers are waiting','When the bus has already arrived','When staff want to conceal all uncertainty'],'especially when conditions are variable','A realistic range can communicate uncertain conditions better than false precision.'),
q('Sentence Simplification','Which statement best expresses the point of the last paragraph?',['Improving service and informing users solve related but different problems','A display is enough to improve the operation of every bus','Timing data should replace passenger feedback','Communication matters only when service cannot improve'],'operational improvements and communication address different aspects','The paraphrase preserves the distinction without dismissing either approach.'),
q('Purpose','Why use two passengers with the same wait?',['To isolate the effect of information from the duration itself','To show that both always feel equally frustrated','To compare bus fares','To argue that uncertain schedules attract passengers'],'even if both eventually wait the same amount of time','Holding duration constant illustrates how information changes experience.')]),
...revisions,
academic('21','Clues in an animal track',`A footprint can identify more than the presence of an animal. Its position relative to other prints may suggest direction and gait, while its depth can reflect both the animal's weight and the softness of the ground. The same animal can leave different-looking tracks on wet soil, sand, or a firm surface.\nInterpreting a trail therefore requires comparing clues rather than treating one mark as decisive. A deep print does not necessarily indicate a heavier animal if the ground beneath it was softer. Overlapping tracks may also come from different times. Rain or wind can alter older marks, making them harder to compare with recent ones.\nTrackers can record surface conditions and measure several prints along a trail. Photographs with a scale preserve size information, but a picture may not show the ground's resistance when the print was made. Combining the visible pattern with knowledge of the substrate supports a more cautious interpretation. The aim is to identify the explanation best supported by the available traces, while keeping alternatives in view.`,[
q('Main Idea','What approach to tracks does the author recommend?',["Interpret several clues in relation to ground conditions", "The depth of one footprint provides the most direct estimate of an animal’s weight", "Photographs are sufficient to reconstruct the ground’s resistance when a track formed", "Track direction is determined mainly by the age of the oldest visible footprint"],'comparing clues rather than treating one mark as decisive','The argument emphasizes contextual interpretation.'),
q('Factual','What can relative print positions suggest?',['Direction and gait','The exact time of rainfall','The animal’s diet','The observer’s distance from the trail'],'may suggest direction and gait','Arrangement is evidence about movement, not the other listed features.'),
q('Inference','Why might two prints from one animal have different depths?',['The ground can vary in softness','The animal must belong to two species','The photographs must use different scales','The prints must have been made in different years'],'both the animal\'s weight and the softness of the ground','Depth has more than one cause, so varying substrate can explain variation.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “substrate” mean here?',['The surface material beneath the animal','The sequence of photographs','The age of an overlapping print','The animal’s pattern of movement'],'wet soil, sand, or a firm surface','The later term refers to the material on which tracks form.'),
q('Purpose','Why mention what a photograph cannot show?',['To identify a limitation of visual records','To reject photographs with scales','To prove all tracks disappear quickly','To prefer weight estimates over gait'],'may not show the ground\'s resistance','The limitation explains why contextual evidence complements images.')]),
academic('22','Making a public map',`A public map simplifies space so that users can find what they need. A transit map may enlarge the city centre and straighten a route, even though these changes distort geographic distances. Such choices can make interchanges clearer. The map is successful if it helps with the intended journey, not if every feature is drawn at the same scale.\nSimplification also creates risks. A traveller may assume two nearby-looking stations are within a short walk when the diagram does not preserve distance. Clear labels and an explanation of scale can reduce this misunderstanding. A separate geographic map may be needed for walking.\nAccessibility adds further design requirements. Distinguishing routes only by colour can make them difficult for some users to identify. Combining colour with names, symbols, or line patterns provides more than one cue. A useful map therefore depends on both the information selected and the ways that information is communicated. Testing it with actual users can expose confusions that seem invisible to its designer.`,[
q('Main Idea','What makes a simplified public map useful?',["Design choices that match user tasks and communicate clearly", "A consistent geographic scale is the main requirement for every transit diagram", "Colour differences provide the clearest route information without additional labels", "Straightened routes are chiefly intended to help travellers estimate walking distances"],'the information selected and the ways that information is communicated','Purpose, clarity, and accessibility guide the design.'),
q('Factual','Why may a transit map straighten routes?',['To clarify connections and interchanges','To shorten the actual train journey','To make every station walkable','To remove the need for route labels'],'make interchanges clearer','Diagrammatic changes aid reading without changing the transport system.'),
q('Inference','Why might a walking traveller need another map?',['The transit diagram may distort distances','The transit map contains no stations','Every route name is unreliable','Colour prevents all geographic representation'],'the diagram does not preserve distance','Walking requires geographic information that a schematic may omit.'),
q('Reference','What does “Such choices” in paragraph 1 refer to?',['Enlarging the centre and straightening routes','Testing users and adding symbols','Taking a shorter walk between stations','Naming the city’s districts'],'enlarge the city centre and straighten a route','The phrase points back to the stated simplifications.'),
q('Purpose','Why recommend combining colour with other cues?',['To support users who cannot easily distinguish colours','To make all routes share a name','To avoid testing the design','To restore exact geographic distances'],'difficult for some users to identify','Redundant visual cues improve identification without requiring colour alone.')]),
academic('23','Restoring a damaged text',`An old manuscript may survive with missing words, faded ink, or torn pages. Editors preparing a readable version sometimes propose restorations using the remaining letters and the author's usual language. A plausible restoration is an interpretation, however, rather than a newly discovered piece of the original.\nOther copies can provide evidence, but they are not automatically independent. Two manuscripts may share an error because one was copied from the other or both came from the same earlier copy. Counting identical readings without considering this relationship can exaggerate their support. Editors examine patterns of differences to understand how versions may be connected.\nA careful edition distinguishes visible text from supplied wording and explains uncertain decisions in notes. This lets readers assess the evidence instead of receiving a smooth text that conceals its gaps. An edition designed for general reading may present fewer notes than a scholarly one, but it should still avoid implying certainty where the source does not provide it. Readability and transparency need to be balanced rather than treated as identical goals.`,[
q('Main Idea','What does careful textual restoration require?',["Separating interpretation from surviving evidence", "Agreement among copies provides sufficient evidence for restoring any missing wording", "Producing a smooth readable text is the main test of a restoration’s accuracy", "Scholarly editions should select a single reconstruction rather than disclose uncertain decisions"],'distinguishes visible text from supplied wording','The passage emphasizes transparent decisions and uncertain evidence.'),
q('Factual','How can two copies share an error?',['They may derive from a common earlier copy','They must be independent witnesses','They were always written by the original author','They contain no readable letters'],'both came from the same earlier copy','A shared source can transmit the same error.'),
q('Inference','Why can counting identical readings exaggerate support?',['Related copies may repeat one source rather than provide independent evidence','All copied words necessarily differ','More copies always create new facts','Notes make readings impossible to compare'],'they are not automatically independent','Dependence reduces the force of repeated agreement.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “supplied” mean in paragraph 3?',['Added by an editor to fill a gap','Written visibly in the surviving source','Purchased from a manuscript owner','Copied without any uncertainty'],'visible text from supplied wording','The contrast distinguishes surviving words from proposed restoration.'),
q('Purpose','Why include notes on uncertain decisions?',['To let readers evaluate the basis for restorations','To prove that the source had no damage','To replace all readable text','To count every copy as independent'],'lets readers assess the evidence','Notes expose decisions that a seamless version might conceal.')]),
academic('24','The uneven spread of an innovation',`A new practice rarely spreads through a community at a uniform rate. Some people try it early, while others wait to observe results. Adoption can depend on cost, available support, and whether the practice fits existing routines. A useful technique may spread slowly if trying it involves substantial risk.\nCommunication networks affect what people learn. An early adopter who is trusted by neighbours may provide more persuasive evidence than a distant advertisement. Yet a network can also circulate an incomplete account, especially when successful cases receive more attention than failures. Observing apparent popularity does not establish that a practice works equally well for everyone.\nResearchers studying diffusion therefore examine both information and circumstances. Two households hearing the same recommendation may make different choices because they have different resources. [A] One might have spare equipment to test a method, whereas another cannot afford a failed trial. [B] These differences help explain why awareness does not always lead to adoption. [C] A sound evaluation asks who can benefit under which conditions. [D] Access to support may matter as much as awareness of the technique.`,[
q('Main Idea','What explains unequal adoption of a practice?',["Differences in networks, resources, and fit with routines", "A trusted recommendation makes practical resources less important to adoption", "The rate of adoption chiefly reflects how effectively advertisements demonstrate success", "Apparent popularity provides the strongest evidence that a practice fits different households"],'both information and circumstances','Knowledge interacts with practical ability and perceived risk.'),
q('Factual','Who can provide persuasive evidence in a community?',['A trusted early adopter','Only a distant advertiser','Only someone who has never tried the practice','Every popular account regardless of accuracy'],'An early adopter who is trusted by neighbours','Trust in a local user can make their experience persuasive.'),
q('Inference','Why might two informed households make different decisions?',['Their capacity to absorb the risk may differ','They must have heard different recommendations','The innovation cannot be useful to either','Awareness guarantees the same response'],'cannot afford a failed trial','Resource differences can change what is feasible.'),
q('Select the Sentence','Which labeled sentence gives a concrete example of unequal resources?',['Sentence A','Sentence B','Sentence C','Sentence D'],'One might have spare equipment to test a method','A describes the contrast; B explains its implication and C states an evaluation principle.'),
q('Purpose','Why mention the prominence of successful cases?',['To warn that circulating accounts can be selective','To prove failures never occur','To require everyone to adopt quickly','To show advertisements are always inaccurate'],'successful cases receive more attention than failures','Selective attention can distort the apparent evidence.')]),
academic('25','Life on a temporary pond',`A temporary pond fills during a wet period and later dries. Its inhabitants must either complete vulnerable life stages while water remains or survive the dry interval in another form. Some organisms produce resistant stages that persist in sediment until suitable conditions return. The absence of visible activity during drought therefore does not necessarily mean the habitat has lost all life.\nThe duration of water availability can vary greatly between years. A species that succeeds during a long wet season may fail when the pond dries unusually early. Studying one year alone can give a misleading impression of a stable community. Repeated observations reveal how persistence depends on both life history and environmental timing.\nTemporary ponds may also contain fewer predators that require permanent water, although this does not make them risk-free. Changing the depth or drainage of a pond can alter its drying pattern and thus its community. Conservation needs to consider the cycle of filling and drying, rather than assume that keeping water present continuously is always an improvement.`,[
q('Main Idea','What is the central argument about temporary ponds?',["Their ecological value depends on a cycle that species have adapted to", "Maintaining permanent water is the most effective way to protect temporary-pond communities", "A successful wet season provides a reliable description of a pond’s long-term community", "The absence of visible activity during drought indicates that organisms must recolonize from elsewhere"],'the cycle of filling and drying','The pond’s temporary nature shapes survival and conservation needs.'),
q('Factual','How can some organisms survive a dry period?',['As resistant stages in sediment','By requiring permanent water predators','By remaining visibly active throughout drought','By preventing all evaporation'],'resistant stages that persist in sediment','The passage identifies a dormant survival strategy.'),
q('Inference','Why might one year’s survey be misleading?',['Water duration and species success vary between years','The pond cannot contain more than one species','All predators arrive only during surveys','Sediment disappears every summer'],'can vary greatly between years','A single season may not represent the range of conditions.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “vulnerable” mean here?',['Susceptible to harm','Permanently inactive','Visible from a distance','Fully independent of water'],'complete vulnerable life stages while water remains','Certain stages depend on water and are at risk when it dries.'),
q('Purpose','Why question continuous water as an improvement?',['It can change the environmental cycle supporting the existing community','It prevents any pond from having life','It always increases drought','It makes repeated observation unnecessary'],'can alter its drying pattern and thus its community','Changing a key cycle can change ecological conditions, even if water seems beneficial.')]),
academic('26','Evidence in a classroom discussion',`A classroom discussion can reveal understanding that a written answer does not show. A student may state a correct result but be unable to explain how it was reached. Another may make an error while offering reasoning that shows substantial progress. Listening to explanations helps a teacher distinguish these cases.\nParticipation is nevertheless an uneven source of evidence. Some students speak readily, while others need time to formulate an answer or prefer to write first. Silence does not establish that a student lacks understanding. A teacher can allow a brief individual response before inviting discussion, creating more routes through which students demonstrate their thinking.\nThe aim is not simply to maximize the number of voices heard. Contributions need to be connected to the learning goal and interpreted in context. Recording who speaks may help identify opportunities to participate, but it is not a direct measure of who has learned most. Combining discussion with other tasks offers a fuller account than treating visible confidence as knowledge.`,[
q('Main Idea','What caution does the author offer about classroom discussion?',["Participation must be interpreted alongside reasoning and other evidence", "Speaking more frequently is the most useful evidence of a student’s reasoning ability", "Written preparation is primarily a method of ensuring that oral responses are correct", "Monitoring participation provides a direct comparison of how much students have learned"],'visible confidence as knowledge','Speech frequency and confidence are not equivalent to demonstrated understanding.'),
q('Factual','What can explanations help teachers distinguish?',['Correct results without understanding and errors with useful reasoning','Students who own different textbooks','The amount of time every task requires','Written answers from spoken sounds'],'unable to explain how it was reached','Explaining provides evidence about process beyond correctness alone.'),
q('Inference','Why offer time to write before speaking?',['Some students need another route to formulate their ideas','It makes all oral discussion unnecessary','It ensures every student has the same confidence','It converts silence into proof of ignorance'],'need time to formulate an answer','Preparation supports expression without assuming immediate speech is the only evidence.'),
q('Reference','What does “these cases” in the first paragraph mean?',['The different relationships between result and reasoning','Students speaking in different languages','The written tasks assigned after discussion','Only students who reach a correct result'],'Another may make an error while offering reasoning','The teacher distinguishes two contrasting patterns of answer and understanding.'),
q('Purpose','Why separate speaking counts from learning measures?',['To avoid treating participation frequency as a direct knowledge score','To discourage monitoring access to discussion','To eliminate learning goals','To replace explanations with attendance'],'not a direct measure of who has learned most','A count can reveal participation opportunities but not fully establish learning.')]),
academic('27','The meaning of a historical price',`A historical price tells a reader how much money was exchanged, but its significance depends on context. A loaf costing a small number of coins might have been expensive relative to a worker's daily earnings. Comparing prices across periods without considering wages and available goods can therefore misrepresent living conditions.\nRecords may also describe different versions of a product. Size, quality, and unit of measurement can change. A lower listed price need not mean that buyers received the same item more cheaply. Researchers try to identify what was being sold before drawing a comparison. When the evidence is incomplete, a range of plausible interpretations may be more appropriate than a single confident conclusion.\nPrices are useful precisely because they can be connected to other evidence. Wage records, household budgets, and descriptions of shortages help explain what a payment meant to different people. A price series can show a pattern, but the social experience behind that pattern requires more than the numbers alone. Economic history combines quantitative comparison with attention to the conditions that made exchanges possible.`,[
q('Main Idea','How should historical prices be interpreted?',["In relation to wages, product characteristics, and other context", "Nominal prices provide the most direct comparison of living conditions in different periods", "Product characteristics matter chiefly when wage records are unavailable", "A continuous price series can replace evidence about the goods that were exchanged"],'its significance depends on context','The passage explains several limits of isolated price comparisons.'),
q('Factual','What can make a loaf expensive despite a small coin price?',['Low daily earnings','A newer price record','An unchanged measurement unit','A larger number of surviving budgets'],'expensive relative to a worker\'s daily earnings','Affordability depends on income relative to cost.'),
q('Inference','Why might a falling listed price not indicate a better bargain?',['The product’s size or quality may have fallen','Wages must have risen at the same time','Records cannot describe products','Every historical coin had equal purchasing power'],'Size, quality, and unit of measurement can change','A valid comparison requires identifying what buyers received.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “series” mean in paragraph 3?',['An ordered set of price observations','A type of household product','An agreement among workers','A single coin payment'],'A price series can show a pattern','Repeated observations can reveal a trend unlike one isolated price.'),
q('Purpose','Why mention household budgets and shortages?',['They help explain the lived significance of a price','They make wage evidence unnecessary','They guarantee an exact interpretation','They show quantitative records cannot be useful'],'help explain what a payment meant to different people','Additional sources connect numerical patterns to experience.')]),
academic('28','How a filter is evaluated',`A water filter can be evaluated by measuring what enters it and what leaves it. A reduction in a selected substance shows performance under the tested conditions, but it does not establish that the filter removes every possible contaminant. Different materials and processes target different properties.\nPerformance can also change over time. A new filter may work well before its capacity is used up. Testing only immediately after installation can therefore overstate what users will receive during extended use. Researchers examine flow rate, replacement intervals, and the characteristics of the incoming water as well as removal levels.\nA comparison between filters should specify both the target and the test conditions. A device suited to one source of water may perform differently with another. Instructions about maintenance are part of the practical system, not merely an accessory to the technical design. Reliable evaluation connects laboratory measurements to the conditions under which people will actually use the device, while stating which questions the test has not addressed.`,[
q('Main Idea','What makes a filter evaluation reliable?',["Specified targets, conditions, time span, and maintenance needs", "Testing a new filter provides the most useful estimate of its performance throughout use", "A device’s removal rate is a sufficient basis for comparison regardless of water source", "Maintenance instructions chiefly explain how to reproduce the original laboratory conditions"],'both the target and the test conditions','The evaluation must define scope and reflect practical use.'),
q('Factual','Why can an installation-only test overstate performance?',['The filter’s capacity may decline during use','Every new filter has already failed','Flow rate is impossible to measure','Maintenance begins only after testing'],'before its capacity is used up','Initial performance may not represent a device near replacement.'),
q('Inference','What does reducing one tested substance establish?',['Performance for that target in those conditions','Removal of all contaminants in every source','That maintenance is optional','That other filters cannot work'],'under the tested conditions','The evidence has a specified scope and cannot be generalized to untested targets.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “intervals” refer to here?',['Periods between replacements','Spaces between filter materials','Differences between contaminant names','Laboratory rooms used for comparison'],'replacement intervals','The term concerns maintenance timing.'),
q('Purpose','Why describe instructions as part of the system?',['User maintenance can affect practical performance','Written instructions replace filtration','All designs require the same replacement schedule','The target substance is chosen by users alone'],'part of the practical system','Technical capability depends in part on use and upkeep.')]),
academic('29','The use of a public clock',`A public clock can coordinate people who do not carry individual timepieces. Before widely available personal watches, a clock in a visible tower could help organize markets, meetings, and work. Its importance depended not only on accuracy but also on whether people could see or hear it from the places where they needed to act.\nDifferent clocks in the same town could show different times. Maintaining a common reference required adjustment and agreement about which clock to follow. [A] A timetable based on one clock might confuse someone relying on another. [B] Coordination therefore involved a shared practice as well as a mechanical device. [C] The machine could display a time, but people had to treat it as authoritative. [D] Agreement can be fragile when institutions maintain separate reference clocks.\nChanges in transport and communication increased the need to coordinate across larger distances. A local convention that worked within one market might be insufficient for a journey connecting several towns. The history of timekeeping thus illustrates how a technical object gains social usefulness through access, agreement, and the scale of the activity it serves.`,[
q('Main Idea','What is the passage mainly explaining?',["A clock’s coordinating value depends on shared use and context", "Accurate tower mechanisms were the main requirement for extending timekeeping across towns", "Personal watches primarily improved coordination by removing the need for a common reference", "Local timetables influenced public clocks more through design than through shared agreement"],'access, agreement, and the scale of the activity','The device works socially through visibility and a common reference.'),
q('Factual','What feature besides accuracy affected a tower clock’s usefulness?',['Whether users could see or hear it','Whether every user owned a watch','Whether the market had several towers','Whether the timetable named its maker'],'whether people could see or hear it','Access to the displayed time matters where action is required.'),
q('Inference','Why could multiple town clocks cause confusion?',['Users might rely on different reference times','All clocks displayed identical time','Markets did not need schedules','Every timetable required personal watches'],'Different clocks in the same town could show different times','A shared reference must be agreed upon when displays diverge.'),
q('Select the Sentence','Which labeled sentence explicitly distinguishes shared practice from the device?',['Sentence B','Sentence A','Sentence C','Sentence D'],'Coordination therefore involved a shared practice as well as a mechanical device','B states the distinction; A gives an example, C elaborates authority, and D describes a risk to agreement.'),
q('Purpose','Why discuss travel between several towns?',['To show how a larger coordination scale changes timekeeping needs','To establish that tower clocks were always inaccurate','To suggest local markets no longer existed','To prove watches were available to everyone'],'might be insufficient for a journey connecting several towns','The example connects the required standard to the reach of the activity.')]),
academic('30','Remembering through a monument',`A monument makes a selected account of the past visible in a public place. Its material may be durable, but the meaning people assign to it can change. An event celebrated by one group may be understood differently by another, and later generations may ask questions that its creators did not anticipate.\nThe inscription usually has limited space, encouraging a brief narrative. This can make a complex event seem simpler than the historical evidence suggests. A monument also gains meaning from location: placing it near a civic building can associate its subject with public authority, while moving it changes that relationship.\nHistorians can study monuments both as statements about earlier events and as evidence of the society that commissioned them. Who paid for a monument, which figures were included, and which voices were absent reveal choices in public remembrance. Adding interpretation nearby can make those choices clearer without pretending that a single object tells the entire story. Public memory is an ongoing conversation, rather than a message whose interpretation was permanently fixed when stone was carved.`,[
q('Main Idea','What does the author argue about monuments?',["They express selective histories whose interpretation can change", "The durability of a monument chiefly determines how later generations interpret its subject", "An inscription offers the most complete account when its subject is associated with public authority", "Moving a monument chiefly changes its physical condition rather than its public interpretation"],'the meaning people assign to it can change','The argument concerns selection, context, and continuing interpretation.'),
q('Factual','Why may an inscription simplify an event?',['It has limited space','Stone prevents the use of names','Historians refuse to read inscriptions','Every creator lacks evidence'],'limited space, encouraging a brief narrative','Compression can conceal historical complexity.'),
q('Inference','What can studying a monument’s funding reveal?',['Choices made by the society that commissioned it','An exact account of every participant in the event','That all later viewers agree','That the inscription needs no interpretation'],'reveal choices in public remembrance','Commissioning decisions provide evidence of the commemorating society.'),
q('Reference','What does “that relationship” in paragraph 2 refer to?',['The association between the monument’s subject and public authority','The difference between stone and other material','The distance between historical events in time','The number of voices in an inscription'],'associate its subject with public authority','Moving the monument changes its location-based association.'),
q('Purpose','Why suggest adding interpretation nearby?',['To clarify the choices behind the monument’s narrative','To make the object tell every aspect of the past','To guarantee unanimous approval','To remove evidence about who commissioned it'],'make those choices clearer','Additional context can reveal selection without claiming completeness.')]),
academic('31','A coating for a stored object',`Conservators preparing an object for storage first examine its condition and record existing damage. This record helps distinguish later changes from features already present. [A] They then remove loose dust with a method suitable for the material, avoiding actions that could dislodge fragile surfaces. [B] The clean object can receive a protective coating if tests show that the coating is appropriate. [C] Storage begins with placing the object in a support that prevents unnecessary movement. [D]\nThe sequence matters because later steps depend on earlier preparation. A coating applied over loose dirt can trap material against the surface, while a support fitted without examining fragile areas may exert pressure where it is unsafe. Each treatment should therefore have a stated purpose rather than follow a routine automatically.\nThe storage environment requires continuing attention as well. Stable support cannot prevent every effect of fluctuating conditions. Staff keep inspection records so they can notice changes and reconsider arrangements when evidence indicates a problem. Preservation is an ongoing process, not a result guaranteed by one initial treatment.`,[
q('Factual','Why record existing damage before treatment?',['To distinguish new changes from earlier features','To avoid all future inspections','To prove the object needs a coating','To select a storage room without examination'],'distinguish later changes from features already present','An initial condition record supplies a comparison for later observations.'),
q('Insert Text','Where best insert: “Only after this coating has dried should storage begin.”?',['Position C','Position A','Position B','Position D'],'The clean object can receive a protective coating','At C, “this coating” has just been introduced and “storage begins” follows. A and B precede the coating; D places the drying requirement after storage has already begun.'),
q('Inference','Why might applying a coating too early be harmful?',['It could trap loose dirt against the surface','It would erase the written condition record','It would eliminate the need for a support','It would stabilize the room temperature'],'can trap material against the surface','Cleaning must precede coating to avoid enclosing loose dirt.'),
q('Vocabulary','What does “dislodge” mean here?',['Move out of place','Make more visible','Record in writing','Protect from movement'],'could dislodge fragile surfaces','The concern is disturbing vulnerable material during dust removal.'),
q('Purpose','Why describe preservation as ongoing?',['Conditions and objects require continued monitoring','Initial preparation has no value','Every coating must be applied daily','Supports always prevent environmental damage'],'Staff keep inspection records','Continued observation can reveal problems and support revised arrangements.')]),
academic('32','The information in an empty shelf',`A shop's empty shelf can indicate several different situations. Demand may have exceeded supply, a delivery may be late, or the shop may have stopped selling the item. A photograph of the shelf alone cannot identify which explanation is correct. Researchers studying availability need information about ordering, deliveries, and sales as well as what is visible at one moment.\nThe timing of observation also matters. A shelf photographed just before restocking may look different shortly afterward. Repeated visits can reveal whether shortages persist or occur only at particular times. Asking staff can provide context, although recollections may be less precise than dated records.\nComparing shops requires similar definitions. One store may display a substitute beside a missing product, while another keeps the category empty. Whether a substitute counts as availability depends on the research question and on whether customers can use it. A clear evaluation states what it measures, collects evidence at suitable times, and avoids treating every absence as proof of the same underlying cause.`,[
q('Main Idea','What does the passage recommend for studying product availability?',["Combine observations with context and consistent definitions", "Repeated shelf visits are sufficient to identify the cause of every product shortage", "Substitute products provide the most consistent measure of availability across shops", "Staff accounts should replace dated records because they include the context of sales"],'collects evidence at suitable times','Multiple causes and observation conditions require careful evidence.'),
q('Negative Factual','Which explanation for an empty shelf is NOT mentioned?',['Customers moved the product to a different aisle','Demand exceeded supply','A delivery was delayed','The shop stopped selling the item'],'Demand may have exceeded supply','The first paragraph lists demand, delivery, and discontinuation, but not customer relocation.'),
q('Inference','Why might repeated visits improve an evaluation?',['They can distinguish persistent shortages from temporary gaps','They make staff memories exact','They guarantee a shop will restock','They remove the need to define substitutes'],'whether shortages persist or occur only at particular times','Repeated observation addresses variation over time.'),
q('Reference','What does “it” in “whether customers can use it” refer to?',['A substitute product','The photograph','A dated sales record','The empty category'],'Whether a substitute counts as availability','The sentence asks whether the substitute meets customer needs.'),
q('Purpose','Why contrast two shops’ treatment of substitutes?',['To show that a comparison depends on its definition of availability','To recommend that every shelf remain empty','To prove one shop has better sales records','To claim substitutes always meet customer needs'],'depends on the research question','The example illustrates why consistent criteria matter.')]),
];
